```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router7

diagnosis_text:
The root cause is router7's eth3 interface being administratively/physically shut
down — NOT an ospfd crash. ospfd on router7 is fully healthy: the process is
running (PID 70, supervised by watchfrr), and router7 currently holds three Full
OSPF adjacencies on eth2 (192.168.10.0/24 → router1), eth4 (172.30.1.0/24 →
router16), and eth5 (172.25.1.0/24 → router8).

eth3 — the link_wan_10_0_0 segment connecting router7 to router10 — is DOWN
(kernel state DOWN, ethtool reports "Link detected: no"). Router10's eth2 is UP
and healthy on its side, confirming the fault is local to router7's eth3. Because
eth3 is down, ospfd cannot send OSPF Hellos on the 10.0.0.0/24 segment; router10's
dead timer expired and its adjacency with router7 was torn down (show ip ospf
interface eth2 on router10 confirmed Neighbor Count = 0). This is the "OSPF
neighbor going down" event the team observed in router7's logs.

The traffic impact is visible in router7's routing table: the 10.0.0.0/24 prefix
is no longer directly connected but instead learned via the detour path through
router8/router17 (OSPF cost 40 instead of 10), and 192.168.144.0/24 (Campus 2
services via router10) is also detoured through that same longer path. This
explains the elevated latency without a full outage — alternative OSPF paths
exist but are suboptimal.

Restarting ospfd will not fix anything, as ospfd never crashed. The correct fix
is to bring router7's eth3 back up:
  ip link set eth3 up
Once the interface is restored, OSPF will re-form the Full adjacency with router10,
the direct low-cost routes will return, and latency will normalize.
=== END ===
```